---
/**
 * Old versus new regime for salaried people. The slab tables come from rates.ts and every
 * break-even figure from break-even.ts, so the page moves when the law (and the engine) does.
 */
import DocLayout from '../layouts/DocLayout.astro';
import ChartLine from '../components/ChartLine.astro';
import { SITE } from '../config/site';
import { bracketLabel, bracketPath, bracketInput } from '../lib/brackets';
import { formatRupees } from '../lib/format';
import { breakEven, rentToBreakEven } from '../lib/tax/break-even';
import { calculate } from '../lib/tax/calculate';
import {
  NEW_REGIME_SLABS,
  OLD_REGIME_SLABS,
  REBATE,
  STANDARD_DEDUCTION,
  type Slab,
} from '../lib/tax/rates';

const slabRows = (slabs: readonly Slab[]) =>
  slabs.map((slab, i) => {
    const from = i === 0 ? 0 : slabs[i - 1].upTo;
    return {
      band:
        slab.upTo === Infinity
          ? `Above ${formatRupees(from)}`
          : `${formatRupees(from === 0 ? 0 : from + 1)} to ${formatRupees(slab.upTo)}`,
      rate: slab.rate === 0 ? 'Nil' : `${Math.round(slab.rate * 100)}%`,
    };
  });

const SALARIES = [14, 16, 18, 20, 24, 30, 40, 50];
const rows = SALARIES.map((lpa) => {
  const input = bracketInput(lpa);
  return { lpa, even: breakEven(input), rent: rentToBreakEven(input) };
});
const plateau = rows.find((r) => r.lpa === 40)!.even.extraNeeded!;
const taxFreeUpTo = REBATE.new.incomeLimit + STANDARD_DEDUCTION.new;

// Tax a year under each regime across salaries: the old regime as it comes (PF under section 123
// only), and with every capped deduction filled, but no HRA.
const CHART = [6, 8, 10, 12, 14, 16, 18, 20, 24, 30, 40, 50];
const FULL = {
  sec80C: 1_50_000,
  nps80CCD1B: 50_000,
  healthInsurance: 25_000,
  selfOccupiedInterest: 2_00_000,
};
const chart = CHART.map((lpa) => {
  const plain = calculate(bracketInput(lpa));
  const full = calculate(bracketInput(lpa, { deductions: FULL }));
  return {
    lpa,
    newTax: plain.regimes.new.tax.totalTax,
    oldTax: plain.regimes.old.tax.totalTax,
    oldFull: full.regimes.old.tax.totalTax,
  };
});
---

<DocLayout
  title={`Old or new tax regime in ${SITE.shortYearLabel}`}
  description={`Old versus new income tax regime for salaried people in ${SITE.shortYearLabel}: both slab tables, and exactly how much in deductions the old regime needs to win at every salary.`}
  updated="2026-10-09"
  published="2026-10-04"
  ogImage="old-vs-new-tax-regime"
  about={['Old tax regime', 'New tax regime', 'Income tax in India']}
  deva="कर"
>
  <p>
    The new regime is the default for {SITE.taxYearLabel}, and for most salaried people it is also
    the cheaper one. It has lower rates across the middle, a {formatRupees(STANDARD_DEDUCTION.new)}{' '}
    standard deduction, and a rebate that makes a salary of up to about {formatRupees(taxFreeUpTo)}{' '}
    tax-free. The old regime keeps its deductions (HRA, section 123 which was 80C, health insurance,
    home loan interest) but charges more on the income that is left.
  </p>
  <p>
    The useful question is not which regime is better in general, but how much you would have to
    claim for the old one to win at your salary. That number is below.
  </p>

  <ChartLine
    label="From 6 to 50 LPA the new regime never costs more than the old one, even when the old regime claims every capped deduction. Only a large HRA exemption on top can change that."
    caption="Income tax a year, with the calculator's standard structure. The dashed line fills section 123, the extra ₹50,000 for NPS, ₹25,000 of health insurance and ₹2 lakh of home loan interest, with no HRA. Even then the new regime costs less at every salary shown; the old regime needs an HRA exemption as well, as the table below works out."
    series={[
      {
        label: 'New regime',
        color: 'var(--color-accent)',
        points: chart.map((c) => ({ x: c.lpa, y: c.newTax })),
      },
      {
        label: 'Old regime, PF only',
        color: 'var(--color-chart-tax)',
        points: chart.map((c) => ({ x: c.lpa, y: c.oldTax })),
      },
      {
        label: 'Old regime, every capped deduction',
        color: 'var(--color-saffron-strong)',
        dashed: true,
        points: chart.map((c) => ({ x: c.lpa, y: c.oldFull })),
      },
    ]}
    xTicks={[6, 10, 14, 20, 30, 40, 50]}
  />

  <h2>The two slab tables</h2>
  <div class="grid gap-8 sm:grid-cols-2">
    <div>
      <h3>New regime (section 202)</h3>
      <table>
        <thead>
          <tr>
            <th scope="col">Taxable income</th>
            <th scope="col">Rate</th>
          </tr>
        </thead>
        <tbody>
          {slabRows(NEW_REGIME_SLABS).map((r) => (
            <tr>
              <td>{r.band}</td>
              <td>{r.rate}</td>
            </tr>
          ))}
        </tbody>
      </table>
      <p>
        Standard deduction {formatRupees(STANDARD_DEDUCTION.new)}. A rebate of up to{' '}
        {formatRupees(REBATE.new.maxRebate)} makes taxable income up to{' '}
        {formatRupees(REBATE.new.incomeLimit)} tax-free, with{' '}
        <a href="/marginal-relief">marginal relief</a> just above it.
      </p>
    </div>
    <div>
      <h3>Old regime, below 60</h3>
      <table>
        <thead>
          <tr>
            <th scope="col">Taxable income</th>
            <th scope="col">Rate</th>
          </tr>
        </thead>
        <tbody>
          {slabRows(OLD_REGIME_SLABS.below60).map((r) => (
            <tr>
              <td>{r.band}</td>
              <td>{r.rate}</td>
            </tr>
          ))}
        </tbody>
      </table>
      <p>
        Standard deduction {formatRupees(STANDARD_DEDUCTION.old)}. A rebate of up to{' '}
        {formatRupees(REBATE.old.maxRebate)} covers taxable income up to{' '}
        {formatRupees(REBATE.old.incomeLimit)}, with no relief above it. Senior citizens get higher
        exemption limits.
      </p>
    </div>
  </div>
  <p>Both regimes add a 4% health and education cess, and a surcharge above ₹50 lakh.</p>

  <h2>What still counts in the new regime</h2>
  <ul>
    <li>The {formatRupees(STANDARD_DEDUCTION.new)} standard deduction.</li>
    <li>
      Your employer's NPS contribution, up to 14% of Basic plus DA for every employee. This is the
      one real planning lever left in the new regime.
    </li>
  </ul>
  <p>
    HRA, section 123 (80C), health insurance, 80CCD(1B), home loan interest on a self-occupied house
    and professional tax are all ignored.
  </p>

  <h2>How much the old regime needs, salary by salary</h2>
  <p>
    The table uses the calculator's standard structure (Basic at half of CTC, PF at the wage
    ceiling, Karnataka professional tax). "Extra needed" is how much you would have to claim on top
    of what the old regime already gives you, which is the ₹50,000 standard deduction, professional
    tax and your PF under section 123. The capped deductions without HRA (the rest of section 123,
    ₹50,000 of NPS, ₹25,000 of health insurance and ₹2 lakh of home loan interest) can add at most
    about {formatRupees(rows[0].even.cappedHeadroom)}.
  </p>
  <div class="overflow-x-auto">
    <table>
      <thead>
        <tr>
          <th scope="col">CTC</th>
          <th scope="col">New regime tax</th>
          <th scope="col">Extra needed</th>
          <th scope="col">Without HRA?</th>
          <th scope="col">Rent that tips it</th>
        </tr>
      </thead>
      <tbody>
        {rows.map(({ lpa, even, rent }) => (
          <tr>
            <td>
              <a href={bracketPath(lpa)}>{bracketLabel(lpa)}</a>
            </td>
            <td>{formatRupees(even.newTax)}</td>
            <td>{even.extraNeeded === null ? 'n/a' : formatRupees(even.extraNeeded)}</td>
            <td>{even.reachableWithoutHra ? 'Possible' : 'No'}</td>
            <td>
              {rent.monthlyRent === null
                ? 'No rent does'
                : `${formatRupees(rent.monthlyRent)} a month`}
            </td>
          </tr>
        ))}
      </tbody>
    </table>
  </div>
  <p>
    "Rent that tips it" assumes you live in one of the{' '}
    <a href="/hra-exemption">eight cities with the 50% HRA limit</a> and have also filled section
    123, the extra NPS and health insurance, but have no home loan.
  </p>

  <h2>Why the bar stops rising at {formatRupees(plateau)}</h2>
  <p>
    Above ₹24 lakh of taxable income both regimes charge 30% on each extra rupee, so the gap between
    them stops growing. From there on the old regime needs the same {formatRupees(plateau)} of extra
    deductions whatever you earn, until the ₹50 lakh surcharge threshold changes the picture again.
    Because the capped deductions cannot reach that on their own, a high earner only benefits from
    the old regime with a large HRA exemption or a big home loan, and usually both.
  </p>

  <h2>Switching</h2>
  <p>
    A salaried person can choose each year: tell your employer at the start of the year so the right
    tax is deducted from your salary, and you can still pick the other regime when you file your
    return. <a href="/">Try both with your own figures</a>.
  </p>
  <p>
    Sources: Income-tax Act, 2025, sections 19, 123 to 126, 156 and 202; Finance Act 2026, First
    Schedule. <a href="/how-it-works">Full method</a>.
  </p>
</DocLayout>
